---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
const path = '/use-cases/segmentation';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'use-cases/segmentation');
const desc = description(site, 'use-cases/segmentation');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Are tags enough for segmentation?",
    "answer": "Tags can represent maintained preferences or operational states. Document who updates and removes them; use event history when the decision depends on an event or time window."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does segment membership permit sending?",
    "answer": "No. Apply current channel permission and suppression checks separately from commercial targeting."
  },
  {
    "question": "How often should definitions be reviewed?",
    "answer": "Review when source fields, catalog mappings or connected campaigns change, and set an owner and review cadence appropriate to their risk."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should an unused segment be deleted?",
    "answer": "Check references in automations and reporting first. Archive its definition and remove it only when those dependencies are understood."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden py-16 md:py-24"><div class="container">
    <a class="link" href="/use-cases">← All use cases</a>
    <p class="catalog-kicker mt-6">Segmentation</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5 max-w-5xl">Shopify email tools for actionable customer segments</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">A segment should answer a sending decision: who needs this message, which data makes them eligible, and what would remove them? Start with the decision and its exclusions before adding another audience label.</p>
    <a class="btn btn-primary mt-9" href="/apps">Compare the tool catalog</a>
  </div></section>
  <article class="container pb-16 space-y-12">
    <section class="prose-shopify"><h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Write the decision before the query</h2>
      <p>For each segment record its purpose, source fields, lookback window, timezone, owner and connected campaigns. A preference group for newsletter content and a purchase-stage group for automation can overlap legitimately. Neither group establishes marketing permission.</p>
      <p>Shopify describes its customer segments as dynamic lists: customers enter or leave as they meet the criteria. This does not establish that a separate email platform receives every field or evaluates membership at the same time. Record which system computes the audience and when the sending system last refreshed it.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="prose-shopify"><h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Distinguish unknown data from a negative answer</h2>
      <p>A missing quiz answer does not mean the customer dislikes the product. Keep unknown preference distinct from an explicit opt-out from that topic. Record where and when a preference was supplied; a purchased gift is weaker evidence of personal taste than a direct preference.</p>
      <p>For a proposed record-store newsletter, a customer selecting jazz should receive jazz content even if their last purchase was a classical gift. A customer with no preference gets a general selection. Product IDs, variant IDs and renamed categories need an agreed mapping so merchandising changes do not silently empty the audience.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="prose-shopify"><h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Make boolean logic reviewable</h2>
      <p>Write the rule in plain language beside the query. Group alternatives before applying exclusions: a category purchase OR an explicit interest is different from requiring both. Decide how cancelled orders, refunds and guest purchases affect the rule, rather than relying on an ambiguous total-spend field.</p>
      <p>Use boundary fixtures: a matching buyer, a refunded buyer, an unknown preference, a subscriber who withdrew permission and a customer just outside the time window. Check why each is included or excluded. Counts alone cannot reveal whether the right people are present.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="prose-shopify"><h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Resolve message conflicts at the sending step</h2>
      <p>Maintain a conflict table for the actual messages. A new purchase may invalidate a winback offer; a service issue may pause a product promotion. A relevant newsletter can still be useful, so do not impose an unexplained global hierarchy on every audience.</p>
      <p>Check eligibility again close to send time where supported, especially after queue delays. A saved export is a snapshot, not a live suppression system. Plan what happens if the source feed stops: pause affected campaigns or fall back to a message that does not depend on the missing fact.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="prose-shopify"><h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Evaluate tools using a reproducible audience</h2>
      <p>Use the Shopify segment as a reference where it contains the required fields. Compare external candidates such as Klaviyo, Drip and Sequenzy on the exact query, event history, null handling and update delay. Do not assume a tool with more conditions automatically produces a better decision.</p>
      <p>A proposed evaluation compares named synthetic profiles across source and destination, then changes a preference and adds a refund before a queued send. Record discrepancies and their cause. Retire unused segments only after checking their automation dependencies; archive the definition so reporting remains interpretable.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="card"><h2 class="font-serif text-3xl">Reference and related workflows</h2>
      <p>Product reference: <a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/customers/customer-segmentation">Shopify customer segmentation</a>. Workflow recommendations and proposed exercises above are editorial guidance.</p>
      <p><a class="link" href="/use-cases/vip-segments">vip segments</a> · <a class="link" href="/use-cases/welcome-series">welcome series</a></p>
    </section>
  </article>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Segmentation FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
